      "descriptionHtml": "<p>A school enterprise chooses $A_1$ or $A_2$ before demand is $S_1$ or $S_2$. The consequence is profit in thousand USD. Total revenues are $$\\begin{aligned}\n\tR(A_1,S_1)&=20,\n\t&\n\tR(A_1,S_2)&=13,\n\t\\\\\n\tR(A_2,S_1)&=18,\n\t&\n\tR(A_2,S_2)&=15.\n\\end{aligned}$$ The directly supplied total costs are $$\\begin{aligned}\n\tC(A_1,S_1)&=9,\n\t&\n\tC(A_1,S_2)&=8,\n\t\\\\\n\tC(A_2,S_1)&=10,\n\t&\n\tC(A_2,S_2)&=9.\n\\end{aligned}$$</p>",
      "solutionSections": [
        {
          "title": "C1: Interpreting the decision.",
          "contentHtml": "<div class=\"table-card\"><table class=\"data-table\"><caption>Decision elements</caption><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Alternatives</th><td>$A_1$; $A_2$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">States of nature</th><td>$S_1$; $S_2$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Uncertain event</th><td><p>Demand revealed after the enterprise choice</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Consequences/payoffs</th><td><p>Profit in thousand USD for each alternative&ndash;state pair</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
        },
        {
          "title": "C3: Calculating revenue, cost, and payoff.",
          "contentHtml": "\t<div class=\"table-card\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p>Cell</p></th><th scope=\"col\">$R(A_i,S_j)$</th><th scope=\"col\">$C(A_i,S_j)$</th><th scope=\"col\">$P(A_i,S_j)=R(A_i,S_j)-C(A_i,S_j)$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">$(A_1,S_1)$</th><td><p>20</p></td><td><p>9</p></td><td>$20-9=11$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">$(A_1,S_2)$</th><td><p>13</p></td><td><p>8</p></td><td>$13-8=5$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">$(A_2,S_1)$</th><td><p>18</p></td><td><p>10</p></td><td>$18-10=8$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">$(A_2,S_2)$</th><td><p>15</p></td><td><p>9</p></td><td>$15-9=6$</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\t<div class=\"table-card\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p>Alternative</p></th><th scope=\"col\">$S_1$</th><th scope=\"col\">$S_2$</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">$A_1$</th><td><p>11</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">$A_2$</th><td><p>8</p></td><td><p>6</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
        }
      ],
      "narrativeDescriptionHtml": "<p>A school enterprise must commit to either alternative $A_1$ or alternative $A_2$ before learning whether demand will be $S_1$ or $S_2$. Its accounting team used sales forecasts for each choice under each demand outcome to estimate total revenue, and used purchasing and operating budgets to estimate the corresponding total cost. All amounts and the resulting profit are measured in thousand USD.</p>\n<p>If the enterprise selects $A_1$, forecast sales produce total revenue of $20$ under demand state $S_1$ and $13$ under $S_2$; fulfilling those sales is budgeted to cost $9$ and $8$, respectively. If it selects $A_2$, its different sales plan is expected to bring in $18$ under $S_1$ and $15$ under $S_2$, while the resources required by that plan cost $10$ and $9$, respectively. The enterprise must calculate the profit for every possible alternative&ndash;state pair before constructing its payoff table.</p>"
